The quickest way to get a feel for pancake is to watch one program call another.
So let us write the calling half, look at every line, and then point you at the
[surface]($link.page('surfaces')) that matches your language. The C, Rust,
TypeScript, Python, and Racket entry points sit just below the Zig walkthrough.

## In Zig

pancake is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) package, so the first step is to fetch it
into your build:

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake
```

```zig
const pancake = b.dependency("pancake", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
exe.root_module.addImport("pancake", pancake.module("pancake"));
```

The client itself is one object and a handful of verbs. Here is the entire
round-trip:

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
>
> // tcp: the peer is a place. Its name, a cleartext `host:port`, is called the
> // "designator", and `tcp.at` wraps that name as a dialable locator. The slice
> // names the transports this client can reach.
> var tcp_resident = pancake.net.tcp.Resident{ .io = io };
> const partner = pancake.net.tcp.at(designator);
>
> const session = try pancake.driver.connect(
>     allocator,
>     &.{tcp_resident.netlayer()},
>     partner,
>     .ephemeral,
>     // Both budgets default to 0, which means "wait forever". A silent or
>     // unreachable peer would park this program with no way out, so a
>     // client always passes finite ones.
>     .{ .handshake_timeout_ms = 10_000, .settle_timeout_ms = 10_000 },
> );
> defer session.deinit();
>
> const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
> defer arena.deinit();
> const settled = try session.call(greeter, .{
>     .verb = "greet",
>     .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
> }, arena.allocator());
> switch (settled) {
>     .fulfilled => |arg| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{arg.string}),
>     .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
>     // The third outcome: an introduction to a capability on another peer.
>     // A Host presents it there and hands you the live result. This greeter never sends one.
>     .give => {},
> }
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
> // In-repo builds only: `iroh` is wiring beside the package, not part of it.
> // From C and the bindings this transport is `libpancake_net_transport_iroh`.
> const iroh = @import("iroh");
>
> // iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. `.discovery` resolves
> // the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> var iroh_resident = try iroh.Resident.init(io, null, .{ .reachability = .discovery });
> defer iroh_resident.deinit();
> const partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh";
>
> const session = try pancake.driver.connectUriOwned(
>     allocator,
>     &.{iroh_resident.netlayer()},
>     partner,
>     .ephemeral,
>     .{},
> );
> defer session.deinit();
>
> const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
> defer arena.deinit();
> const settled = try session.call(greeter, .{
>     .verb = "greet",
>     .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
> }, arena.allocator());
> switch (settled) {
>     .fulfilled => |arg| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{arg.string}),
>     .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
>     // The third outcome: an introduction to a capability on another peer.
>     // A Host presents it there and hands you the live result. This greeter never sends one.
>     .give => {},
> }
> ```

What the snippet does not spell out:

- A **netlayer** is pancake's word for a transport
  ([Transports]($link.page('transports'))).
- A **locator** is the dialable `ocapn://` address, and the **designator** is
  the peer's name inside it: a `host:port` for `tcp`, a public key for a keyed
  transport ([Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) defines both).
- **`io`** is your `std.Io` instance. The checked example constructs it with
  `var threaded = std.Io.Threaded.init(allocator, .{});` and then
  `const io = threaded.io();`.
- **`designator`** in the snippet is just the address string, for example
  `const designator = "127.0.0.1:9099";`.
- **`.ephemeral`** is the session identity, a throwaway keypair minted for this
  connection. A stable identity would go here instead; the
  [CLI]($link.page('cli'))'s `init` mints and stores one, and the keyed
  transports on [Transports]($link.page('transports')) are where a stable key
  earns its keep.
- The **arena** owns reply allocations; drop it to free them.
- The third settle arm, **`give`**, is an introduction a Host + `driver.Bridge`
  redeems ([Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains it). This greeter never
  sends one.
- The tcp snippet dials with `connect`; the iroh snippet uses `connectUriOwned` on the
  same `Session` path.

The tcp snippet is the checked contract:
[`readme_quick_start.zig`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/readme_quick_start.zig)
carries it verbatim, `zig build examples` runs it, and `zig build meta-check`
holds this page to those lines. The iroh snippet is illustrative. It follows the
[rooms client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/apps/rooms/zig/client.zig):
the same reachability posture, a bare node id, and the same fetch, call, and
switch. The id still arrives out of band. Discovery turns a name you were given
into a route; it does not browse for peers. The
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) page covers dial-by-id in full.

Both halves together:
[`01_connect_and_serve.zig`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig).

Want a live partner to dial while you write this? `zig build` puts the
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) on your path, and
`pancake ocapn listen --publish greeter` prints a locator serving the same
greeter this client fetches.

## From C, or any FFI host

The same library is a plain C ABI in two shapes. To dial, fetch, and call in one
thread, use the owning session (`pancake_tcp_session_connect` through
`pancake_tcp_session_release`). Connect takes the same two budgets the Zig
snippet above passes, and for the same reason: zero means wait forever, so a
client that dials the network gives them finite values. When your program already owns the event loop,
drive the sans-io peer instead: `pancake_peer_recv_stream` in,
`pancake_peer_next_event` and `pancake_peer_take_outbound` out. Either way the
surface is opaque handles, fixed-width return codes, `ptr + len` views, and
explicit release. That is the shape every binding wraps. Build, ownership, and a
first program:
[consuming pancake from C](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md).

## In Rust, TypeScript, Python, or Racket

If you write one of these four, a binding already did the wrapping for you. Each one
is a genuine foreign-language consumer of the C ABI and presents an
idiomatic API on top, so you write the language you already know instead of foreign
function calls. Start at the [bindings overview]($link.page('bindings')) for the
shape they share, then jump to your language:
[Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust')),
[TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts')),
[Python]($link.page('bindings/python')), or
[Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket')).

Writing for the browser instead of a server? The TypeScript binding also
compiles to WebAssembly as `@pancake/web`; the page always dials rather than
listens. Start at [In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')).

## Then go deeper

New to capabilities, locators, pipelining, or three-party handoff? Read
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) first. Those are the hard ideas, once.
When the layer names blur (`Session`, `Host`, `Peer`),
[modules]($link.page('modules')) sorts them out, and
[Surfaces]($link.page('surfaces')) lists every way to hold the library.
